entry beta-particle
term: Beta Particle (β)
image: ../../assets/images/terms/beta particle.webp
related_terms: alpha-particle
related_terms: gamma-ray
related_terms: decay
related_terms: tritium
related_terms: ionizing-radiation
quick_facts: An electron (β⁻) or positron (β⁺) from the nucleus
quick_facts: Stopped by a few mm of plastic or aluminum
quick_facts: Can penetrate slightly beyond dead skin layers
quick_facts: Positron emitters enable PET medical imaging
quick_facts: Emitted across the whole periodic table — e.g. carbon-14, tritium

sources:
    {"organization":"EPA","title":"Radiation Basics","url":"https://www.epa.gov/radiation/radiation-basics"}

definition:
    A high-speed electron (β⁻) or positron (β⁺) thrown out of an unstable nucleus during beta decay — effectively a neutron converting into a proton (or vice versa) inside the nucleus. Beta particles are far more penetrating than alphas: they can slip through skin and paper but stop in a few millimeters of plastic or aluminum. Positron-emitting isotopes like carbon-11 and fluorine-18 power PET medical scans.

debunking_snippet:
    “Any metal shield makes beta radiation harmless.” Dense metals like lead can actually make things worse — betas striking them emit secondary X-rays (bremsstrahlung). Low-density plastic or acrylic is the right shield.
